<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Berea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Berea was fought on 20 December 1852 on the Berea plateau in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesotho">Lesotho</a> between British forces under Sir George Cathcart and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a>. Cathcart's punitive expedition to seize <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> met stiff resistance, and the fighting was indecisive. Moshoeshoe then sent a conciliatory letter, allowing Cathcart to withdraw and claim success.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-berea.jpg" alt="Battle of Berea" width="320" height="547" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enclosed in a dispatch from Governor Cathcart to the Secretary of State for the Colonies. … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_berea_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 1848 Sir Harry Smith, governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, pressed Moshoeshoe into recognising British paramount authority north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river">Orange River</a>, and a series of such treaties established the Orange River Sovereignty. Disputes over borders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> raiding followed, and on 30 June 1851 a force under the British Resident, Major Henry Douglas Warden, was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> and their Taung allies at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-viervoet">Battle of Viervoet</a>. Later demands for the return of stolen cattle and horses were met only in part.</p><p>Having largely suppressed Xhosa resistance in the east, Major-General Sir George Cathcart marched north from Burgersdorp in late November 1852 with some 2,500 men. A committee at Platberg required the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> to hand over 10,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and 1,000 horses within three days. Moshoeshoe asked for more time and warned that &quot;a dog when beaten would show its teeth&quot;; 3,500 cattle were collected, but the British remained adamant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 20 December Cathcart divided his force into three columns to cross the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caledon-river">Caledon River</a> and move round the Berea plateau towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a>, seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> as they went. Moshoeshoe's son Molapo ambushed the northern column under Lieutenant-Colonel G. Napier as it drove off 4,000 captured cattle, and a party of 30 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">Lancers</a> cornered against a stony ridge lost 27 killed.</p><p>The central column under Lieutenant-Colonel William Eyre seized only about 1,500 of a herd of 30,000 before Molapo's horsemen, carrying lances taken from the dead <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lance">Lancers</a>, struck it. Captain Walter Faunce was captured and executed in retaliation for the accidental killing of civilians. Cathcart's own column held a knoll near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaba-bosiu">Thaba Bosiu</a> against up to 6,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> cavalry led by Moshoeshoe and his sons until they withdrew at about 8 p.m.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Short of ammunition, Cathcart fell back to his camp on the Caledon. Moshoeshoe's letter pleading for peace, translated by the French missionary Eugène Casalis, reached him there under a flag of truce, and he accepted a truce rather than risk a larger war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sotho-people">Basotho</a> paid 3,500 head of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a>. British casualties were 38 killed and 14 wounded; modern estimates put Basotho losses at fewer than 50. The Basotho remember it as Ntoa ea Masole, the Battle of the Soldiers, and relations with the British afterwards remained amicable.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Berea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Sovereignty proved costly and unruly, embroiled in land disputes between settlers and the Basotho of Moshoeshoe, and after damaging clashes with the Basotho at Viervoet in 1851 and <b>Berea</b> in 1852 the government in London decided to withdraw.</blockquote><a href="/empire/orange-free-state">Orange Free State · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His boundary lines between Boer farms and the lands of Moshoeshoe's Basotho Kingdom satisfied no one, and British forces were defeated at Viervoet in 1851 and fought an indecisive battle at <b>Berea</b> in 1852.</blockquote><a href="/state/orange-river-sovereignty">Orange River Sovereignty</a></li></ul></section>
